\section*{Maker winning strategy for Snaky on the infinite square lattice}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Snaky within 21 Maker claims}: The paper claims a Maker strategy for Snaky using at most 21 Maker moves against every legal Breaker response. It can operate on a fixed 251-cell region inside a $17\times17$ square.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Snaky within 21 Maker claims}: Finite winning conditions list required Maker cells, a Breaker-free envelope, and a bound on remaining moves. Backward references combine child conditions after one chosen Maker claim, using envelope intersections to ensure that one Breaker move cannot kill every continuation. A 728-condition certificate closes at the empty initial position; induction extracts a legal policy with the stated move bound.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Snaky within 21 Maker claims}: The exact six-cell shape S, allowed lattice isometries and ordinary unbiased Maker-first empty infinite-board rules match the game. The theorem gives a single legal policy winning against every legal Breaker continuation within 21 Maker claims, stronger than a winning strategy without a move bound.

This page records partial support. Complete resolution of the catalogue question is not established by this record.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 187 describes Lean coverage for the infinite-board 21-move policy and older conditional certificates; the smaller-board restriction is not selected.

\textit{Snaky within 21 Maker claims}: The manuscript links \texttt{SnakyCertificate.lean}, \texttt{SnakyConditional.lean}, \texttt{SnakyTwentyOne.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

